// m_infantry.c -- INFANTRY monster (2023 Quake II re-release / "KEX"
// engine). Ported from ~/Projects/quake2-rerelease-dll/rerelease/
// m_infantry.cpp (928 lines, C++17) + m_infantry.h (280 lines, frame index
// constants "generated by ModelGen"). Combines both into one module, per
// this porting round's brief (frame constants + full behavior, one file per
// monster) -- unlike m_flash.ts/m_player.ts/m_rider.ts, which are pure
// frame-index data ports of files whose .cpp sibling has not landed yet.
// Every mframe_t table below is transcribed row-for-row against the C++
// source and constructed through the landed `MmoveT` class, whose `frame`
// setter throws if the row count doesn't match `lastframe - firstframe + 1`
// (see g_local_types.ts) -- every table in this file matches exactly; no
// `allowFrameCountMismatch` exemption was needed.
//
// ============================================================================
// EXTERNAL / CROSS-CUTTING DEPENDENCIES NOT YET PORTED (throwing stubs, cited)
// ============================================================================
// `blocked_checkjump` and `M_MonsterDodge` are declared in g_local.h but
// DEFINED in rogue/g_rogue_newai.cpp (confirmed by grep -- no such symbol
// exists anywhere in the base rerelease/*.cpp tree). Both are genuinely
// large, generic (non-monster-specific) AI subsystems shared verbatim by
// every KEX monster, not specific to infantry:
// - `blocked_jump_result_t blocked_checkjump(edict_t*, float)`
//   (rogue/g_rogue_newai.cpp:123-~230+): branches on `AI_PATHING`/
//   `nav_path`/`drop_height`/`jump_height` with several `gi.trace` calls;
//   genuinely belongs to a future `rogue/g_rogue_newai.ts` unit, matching
//   this port line's own established precedent for out-of-scope ROGUE
//   content (g_ai.ts's `monster_done_dodge` stub, g_turret.ts's
//   `infantry_*` stubs -- see below). Local throwing stub, cited.
// - `MONSTERINFO_DODGE(M_MonsterDodge)` (rogue/g_rogue_newai.cpp:1304-~1400+):
//   depends on `G_SkillCheck` (skill-cvar-gated RNG, also rogue-only) and is
//   ~100 lines of duck/sidestep decision logic. Local throwing stub, cited.
// `infantry_blocked` (MONSTERINFO_BLOCKED) calls `blocked_checkjump` as its
// primary branch and only falls back to `blocked_checkplat` (ported for
// real below -- self-contained, no nav_path dependency) if that returns
// NO_JUMP; `self.monsterinfo.dodge = M_MonsterDodge` is called from
// `infantry_pain`'s pain-debounce/no-react branches (each ~33% of the time,
// via `frandom() < 0.33`) and from `ai_run`'s own dodge integration
// (g_ai.ts). Both throw today when actually reached -- an honest
// consequence of the missing rogue unit, not a silent behavior change,
// mirroring g_turret.ts's own "SP_turret_driver... always throws today"
// precedent for the identical kind of gap. A future `rogue/g_rogue_newai.ts`
// unit should replace both stubs with real imports.
//
// `monster_done_dodge` (g_local.h decl, defined rogue/g_rogue_monster.cpp:98)
// is a 4-line function with no rogue-specific dependencies (just two
// aiflag/attack_state field writes) -- ported for REAL here (not stubbed),
// duplicating the small helper per-file exactly like g_turret.ts duplicates
// `edictFmt`/`frameTimeAsGtime`/`cvarInt`. g_ai.ts ALSO carries its own
// (unexported, throwing) stub of this same symbol at g_ai.ts:321-323; that
// is NOT swapped here since g_ai.ts is out of this unit's file scope --
// exactly the same "not swapped here" treatment g_monster.ts's own header
// gives g_utils.ts's `G_MonsterKilled` stub. A future unit should replace
// g_ai.ts's stub with a real shared import once a canonical home exists.
//
// `monster_footstep`/`M_CheckGib` are `inline` functions defined directly
// in g_local.h (g_local.h:3282, g_local.h:3521), not in any .cpp -- the
// same "PLACEMENT-MISMATCH FUNCTIONS PORTED LOCALLY" situation g_monster.ts's
// own header documents for its own g_local.h inlines. Both are tiny and
// self-contained, so both are ported for real here, duplicated per-file per
// that same established precedent.
//
// `PredictAim` (rogue/g_rogue_newai.cpp:1083-1135) is also rogue-authored,
// but it is load-bearing for `InfantryMachineGun` (the monster's only
// ranged attack) and is small/self-contained (no nav_path dependency) --
// ported for REAL here rather than stubbed, since stubbing it would make
// every infantry gunshot throw.
// `monster_duck_down`/`monster_duck_hold`/`monster_duck_up`
// (rogue/g_rogue_newai.cpp:1424-1445) are likewise small and self-contained
// -- ported for real.
//
// ============================================================================
// KNOWN C++ SOURCE QUIRK, PRESERVED BUG-FOR-BUG
// ============================================================================
// `InfantryMachineGun`'s run-attack flash-number expression
// (m_infantry.cpp:287): `MZ2_INFANTRY_MACHINEGUN_14 + (self->s.frame -
// MZ2_INFANTRY_MACHINEGUN_14)` algebraically simplifies to just
// `self->s.frame` (X + (Y - X) == Y) -- almost certainly meant to read
// `MZ2_INFANTRY_MACHINEGUN_14 + (self->s.frame - FRAME_run201)` (the
// pattern the sibling death-frame branch two lines down actually uses:
// `MZ2_INFANTRY_MACHINEGUN_2 + (self->s.frame - FRAME_death211)`). Kept
// exactly as written per this porting round's bug-for-bug mandate --
// `flash_number` ends up being the raw `s.frame` value reinterpreted as a
// `monster_muzzleflash_id_t`, which happens to still land inside the
// MZ2_INFANTRY_MACHINEGUN_14..21 run range numerically close enough that it
// doesn't crash (`monster_flash_offset` has 290 entries), but is not the
// intended flash-position lookup.
//
